<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Jafar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1691–<a href="/years/1765">1765</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Jafar was the commander of the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj ud-Daulah</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, who secretly allied with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and held back his troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757. Installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> as nawab, he depended on the Company, which replaced him in 1760 with his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> and restored him in 1763. His name became a byword for treachery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-jafar.jpg" alt="Mir Jafar" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Jafar_(left)_and_Mir_Miran_(right).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
